# Argus environment file. Copy to `.env` (gitignored) and fill in.
#
#   cp .env.example .env && chmod 600 .env
#
# This is the ONLY place credentials exist at runtime. They are never written to
# a profile config (argus.<profile>.toml), never baked into the Docker image, and
# never passed on a command line (where `ps` would expose them).
# docker-compose.yml loads this file via `env_file:`, so the values are injected
# into the container at start.
#
# On the droplet this file lives at /opt/argus/.env, owned by root, mode 600.
# It is the single highest-value secret on the box — a refresh token grants
# account access until it is revoked.
#
# IT IS NOT TOUCHED BY THE CUTOVER. Both worlds' credentials live here at the
# same time, and the profile (/etc/argus/host.env) selects between them — so
# going live, and rolling back, are that one file changing and nothing else.

# ===========================================================================
# TASTYTRADE OAUTH — a (role, world) grid
# ===========================================================================
# These are Argus's OWN tastytrade app registrations — never TOMIC's.
#
# Every variable names BOTH axes: TASTYTRADE_<ROLE>_<WORLD>_<FIELD>.
#
#   ROLE   TRADING can move money. MARKETDATA is read-only quotes/greeks/
#          instruments, and the session layer REFUSES to hand it to account or
#          order code.
#   WORLD  CERT is the sandbox API. LIVE is production. Entirely separate
#          logins — a cert refresh token cannot read live quotes, and a live one
#          cannot authenticate against cert.
#
# Role comes first so that `grep TASTYTRADE_TRADING_` is a complete audit of
# every credential that can reach an order.
#
# Sandbox-first is enforced in code and is unchanged: only environment="live"
# hits the live API. As of 2026-09-09 the CREDENTIAL is keyed on that same
# `environment`, so the endpoint and the credential can no longer disagree.
# -> docs/decisions/2026-09-09-trading-credentials-are-environment-keyed.md

# --- trading / cert --------------------------------------------------------
# ALWAYS required. Every non-live profile (shadow, sandbox, dev) trades on this,
# and market data falls back to it when [marketdata] source != "live".
TASTYTRADE_TRADING_CERT_CLIENT_SECRET=
TASTYTRADE_TRADING_CERT_REFRESH_TOKEN=

# --- trading / live --------------------------------------------------------
# Required only when the loaded profile declares environment = "live". A live
# profile REFUSES TO START without it — and also refuses if it holds the same
# values as the cert slot above, because a cert credential cannot authenticate
# against production and six failed logins ten minutes apart accrue toward
# tastytrade's IP-block threshold.
#
# Fill this in BEFORE the cutover, not during it. That is the whole point: with
# both slots populated, going live is the profile flip alone.
TASTYTRADE_TRADING_LIVE_CLIENT_SECRET=
TASTYTRADE_TRADING_LIVE_REFRESH_TOKEN=

# --- market data / live ----------------------------------------------------
# Required whenever the profile has [marketdata] source = "live" — true in BOTH
# shipped profiles, so in practice always. Sandbox quotes are 15-min delayed:
# fine for mechanics, useless for judging signal quality.
#
# READ-ONLY, and it may be the same production registration as the live trading
# slot above or a different one. If it is the same, revoking it takes both down
# and the read-only/money-moving split holds only in code — `tools.credcheck`
# says which case you are in.
TASTYTRADE_MARKETDATA_LIVE_CLIENT_SECRET=
TASTYTRADE_MARKETDATA_LIVE_REFRESH_TOKEN=

# Only needed to originally mint a refresh token via the OAuth authorization
# flow — NOT consumed by Argus at runtime. Kept here for reference; add a
# second one if the live registration is distinct.
TASTYTRADE_CERT_CLIENTID=
# TASTYTRADE_LIVE_CLIENTID=

# Verify the shape of all of the above without printing any value:
#   ./.venv/bin/python -m argus.tools.credcheck

# ===========================================================================
# KILL-SWITCH CONTROL ENDPOINT
# ===========================================================================
# Bearer token for the always-on POST /kill control surface (spec §6 — the
# phone-reachable kill switch). REQUIRED: the supervisor refuses to start without
# it, because an unauthenticated halt control is a broken safety rail.
#
# It is a SECRET — never in a profile config or code, never logged. Generate a long
# random value, e.g.:  python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(32))"
# The endpoint's bind address (Tailscale IP + port) lives in the profile
# config's [server] section; only the token is here.
#
# Trigger a kill from a phone on the tailnet (one action; see CLAUDE.md):
#   curl -fsS -XPOST -H "Authorization: Bearer $ARGUS_KILL_TOKEN" \
#        http://<tailscale-ip>:<port>/kill
ARGUS_KILL_TOKEN=

# ===========================================================================
# ALERTING (Slack incoming webhook)
# ===========================================================================
# The real path behind `alerts.AlertFn` (src/argus/notify.py). Everything that
# needs to reach a human already calls that seam — audit queue depth and write
# failure, event-loop stalls, reconciliation mismatches, session errors, foreign
# fills, price-walk failures, and every position-watchdog trigger and exit — but
# until this is set they only reach the log.
#
# It IS a credential: an incoming-webhook URL is the whole authentication, so
# anyone holding it can post to the channel. Never in a profile config, never in code,
# and never logged — error messages carry only scheme + host.
#
# OPTIONAL, unlike ARGUS_KILL_TOKEN. A missing webhook is a missing notification
# channel, not a broken safety rail: every alert still reaches the log and every
# rail still functions, so the process starts normally without it. Must be
# https:// — an http:// value is refused rather than sending internal state in the
# clear.
#
# Create one at https://api.slack.com/messaging/webhooks (Incoming Webhooks ->
# Add New Webhook to Workspace) and point it at whichever channel you watch on
# your phone — the same phone that holds the kill-switch Shortcut.
# ARGUS_SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL=

# Where the NIGHTLY EOD DIGEST goes (scripts/argus-eod.sh, 16:45 ET on trading
# days). Same kind of credential as the line above, same optionality — no webhook
# means the report is still rendered and archived, it just does not come to you.
#
# ITS OWN CHANNEL, deliberately. A digest that arrives every evening in the channel
# where Tier 1 pages land teaches you to skim the channel that must never be
# skimmed. Point this at something like #argus-eod and leave the alert webhook for
# alerts. Unset, the script falls back to ARGUS_SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL so a fresh host
# still delivers — the fallback is for bootstrapping, not for steady state.
#
# Job-level FAILURES (the report could not be produced or delivered) always page on
# ARGUS_SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL instead, because that is an alert, not a digest.
# ARGUS_REPORT_SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL=

# ===========================================================================
# RUNTIME / DEPLOYMENT (not secrets — all have safe defaults)
# ===========================================================================

# Which image tag docker-compose runs. This is the rollback lever: set it to a
# previously built tag and `docker compose up -d` reverts code AND every pinned
# dependency in one step. Defaults to `local` (what `docker compose build`
# produces on this machine).
# ARGUS_IMAGE_TAG=local

# Log verbosity for `python -m argus`. Defaults to INFO — here, and again in
# docker-compose.yml (`${ARGUS_LOG_LEVEL:-INFO}`). Leaving it unset on the
# droplet is the recommended setting: it cannot drift from those defaults.
#
# STILL AVOID DEBUG on a deployed box, but no longer because of a token leak.
# `argus/__main__.py` pins the tastytrade, httpx, httpx2, httpcore and websockets
# loggers to WARNING regardless of this level (matching
# argus.bootstrap.configure_logging), so the AlertStreamer subscription frame's
# live `Bearer <session_token>` (site-packages/tastytrade/streamer.py:302) no
# longer reaches the json-file log on disk or journald. Two reasons remain:
# DEBUG is very noisy, and the guarantee only covers the loggers we knew to name
# — a new dependency that logs at DEBUG is not covered.
#
# The dev tools (tools/shadow.py, tools/quotes.py) route through
# argus.bootstrap.configure_logging(), which pins the same loggers unless
# --verbose is passed.
#
# For protocol debugging, prefer:  ARGUS_LOG_LEVEL=INFO + tools/shadow --verbose
# on an interactive session you can watch and discard.
# ARGUS_LOG_LEVEL=INFO

# DO NOT SET ARGUS_CONFIG HERE. An explicit path WINS over the profile
# (config.resolve_config_path), and `env_file:` entries reach the container — so
# a line here would point the process at a file that is no longer mounted, and
# /status could no longer report which profile is armed. The image carries
# neither an ARGUS_CONFIG nor a baked config, deliberately: a drifted mount
# falling back to a baked copy is the one failure the config fingerprint cannot
# see, because both come from one checkout.
#   grep -n ARGUS_CONFIG /opt/argus/.env    # must print nothing

# DO NOT SET ARGUS_ENV HERE EITHER. It names the config FILE (`live` ->
# argus.live.toml), and on the droplet it comes from /etc/argus/host.env —
# root-owned, 600, per host, NOT in git — via argus.service's EnvironmentFile.
# THE LIVE CUTOVER IS THAT ONE FILE CHANGING. Setting it in this hand-edited
# file, which the deploy never touches, is how the wrong config gets deployed
# quietly. It no longer overrides `environment`: the file's own key remains the
# sole authority on trading behaviour and the loader REFUSES when the two
# disagree.
# -> docs/decisions/2026-09-04-config-profiles.md

# ===========================================================================
# DIAGNOSTICS — all OFF by default. Turn on for an investigation, then OFF.
# ===========================================================================
# Both cost real runtime overhead. Neither is a steady-state setting, and
# neither should be left on after the question it was answering is closed.
# They are read at process start, so changing one is a RESTART, and because
# docker-compose loads this file with `env_file:` the restart must recreate
# the container: `sudo systemctl restart argus.service` does exactly that.

# --- Allocation profiler (src/argus/memprofile.py) -------------------------
# Periodic "top allocation sites by growth since startup" reports to the
# journal. Value is the traceback depth: 1/true/yes/on = the default depth (8),
# or an explicit integer. 0/false/no/off/absent = disabled.
#
# COST: roughly DOUBLES process RSS and taxes every allocation. While it is on,
# `/status`'s rss_bytes is NOT comparable to a reading taken without it, and its
# own trace table is not counted in its own diff.
#
# It answers "what Python objects are accumulating". As of 2026-08-06 that
# question is CLOSED for the lag ramp — seven reports across three hours were
# flat to +-20 KiB while loop lag tripled. Leave it off unless hunting a new
# leak.
# ARGUS_TRACEMALLOC=0
# ARGUS_TRACEMALLOC_INTERVAL_S=1800

# --- Event-loop stall attribution (app.enable_loop_debug) ------------------
# Turns on asyncio debug mode, which makes the loop time EVERY callback and log
# any that exceeds the threshold below, WITH ITS IDENTITY:
#   Executing <Task ... coro=<thing() at file:line>> took 1.234 seconds
#
# This is the diagnostic that returns a NAME rather than another exclusion, and
# it is the current path on the intra-session lag ramp (CLAUDE.md): a ~1s block
# of the loop, once per ~11s, growing through a session, cause unknown.
# 1/true/yes/on to enable; anything else (or absent) = off.
#
# COST: debug mode also enables coroutine origin tracking and non-threadsafe
# call checks. Run it a session or two, read the names, turn it off.
# ARGUS_LOOP_DEBUG=0

# Seconds a single callback may hold the loop before it is logged. Default 0.25,
# chosen well above the heartbeat's 0.05s breach threshold: the target is the
# ~1s stall, and logging every 50ms callback on a 2,420 event/sec feed would
# bury it. Must be > 0 — a non-positive or unparseable value falls back to the
# default rather than flooding the journal.
# ARGUS_SLOW_CALLBACK_S=0.25

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FRED API key — OPERATIONAL TOOLING ONLY, never read by the running process.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Used solely by `python -m argus.tools.blackout_dates`, which regenerates the
# macro-event blackout date file `[events]` points at (CPI/NFP/PCE release dates;
# FOMC has no FRED release id and stays hand-maintained in fomc_dates.txt).
#
# `python -m argus` NEVER reads this — it only reads the generated file — so a
# missing key cannot affect a trading session. It is not needed in the container.
#
# This is ARGUS'S OWN key. Do not reuse TOMIC's FRED_API_KEY, same rule as the
# separate tastytrade app registration. Free: https://fredaccount.stlouisfed.org/apikeys
# ARGUS_FRED_API_KEY=
